<Base title="Scaling a relational database" description="Follow reads, writes and failures through larger servers, caches, replicas and shards, and choose an arrangement for the limit you actually have.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Scaling a relational database</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>A database has to fit its data somewhere and complete work quickly enough for its callers. As a service grows, either requirement can become difficult. A table may outgrow the available storage. Queries may compete for CPU or memory. Many requests may queue behind one busy record even while most of the server is idle. These are different limits, and adding the same machine to each arrangement will not have the same effect.</p>
  <p><strong>Scaling</strong> means changing the system so that it can meet a larger demand. Sometimes that means doing less work per request. Sometimes it means giving one database more resources, copying data so more servers can read it, or dividing ownership so several servers can handle independent writes. The useful question is what the added resource allows the next request to do.</p>
  <p>We will follow a service hosting shops for several tenants. Tenant 7 is a small shop; tenant 8 has a much larger catalogue and many more orders. Each checkout writes an order, its lines and stock changes. Catalogue browsing and historical reports contribute many of the reads. This gives us several ways to grow, but each changes where work goes and which failures the application must handle.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#limit">Find the limiting work</a></li><li><a href="#one-server">Use one server well</a></li><li><a href="#copies">Copies for reads</a></li><li><a href="#ownership">Divide the writes</a></li><li><a href="#clusters">What a cluster shares</a></li><li><a href="#failure">Capacity after a failure</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="limit"><h2>Find the limiting work</h2>
   <p>Start with a demand the service must meet. “More customers” is too vague to test. Suppose the target is twice as many completed checkouts per second, with the same acceptable latency and failure rate, while catalogue traffic and a daily report continue. The test needs realistic carts, popular products and tenant sizes. Doubling independent purchases is different from doubling buyers competing for the last mug.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">performance article</a> explains how to separate pool waits, execution work, lock waits and application overhead. For a capacity decision, also watch what changes as the offered load rises. A service may initially complete more requests with little extra delay, then accumulate a queue once its limiting resource is occupied. The growing queue is not additional useful capacity.</p>
   <p>Consider three observations. If reports repeatedly scan the same history, an index, a smaller result or a stored summary may remove work. If independent checkout queries use all available CPU, more compute may help. If all checkouts wait on one stock row, another CPU cannot let them make incompatible decisions about that row at the same time. We may need shorter transactions, fewer accesses to that record, or a different business arrangement for allocating inventory.</p>
   <p>Keep the unit of comparison clear. Measure completed operations and their latency, including timeouts, under the same workload. A larger server that runs one query faster has demonstrated something useful, but has not yet established the throughput of the mixed service. Include maintenance, report refresh and sustained writes in the experiment.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="one-server"><h2>Use one server well</h2>
   <p>Reducing work benefits every arrangement that comes later. Fetch twenty orders instead of an unbounded history. Batch their lines rather than sending twenty separate relationship queries. Use an index whose order matches a selective lookup. Reuse a report result when the report’s freshness contract permits it. Each change has a prediction: fewer exchanges, fewer examined records, or fewer repeated calculations.</p>
   <p>A larger instance can supply more CPU, memory, storage capacity or I/O throughput while keeping one authoritative database. More memory may hold a larger useful working set. Faster storage may reduce a real I/O bottleneck. More cores can serve independent work, although a single serial operation or one contested lock still has its own limit. Choose the resource from the evidence rather than treating instance size as one interchangeable quantity.</p>
   <p>This retains a valuable property for checkout: the order, lines and stock can still be changed under one database transaction and its constraints. It also leaves one server’s operating limits. Check the largest available size, cost, upgrade procedure and recovery time before relying on another size increase. The time needed to restore a large database matters even when ordinary requests are fast.</p>
   <p>Dividing a large table into local partitions can help manage that one server. Orders partitioned by month let an October query exclude November’s partition and let retention operate on a whole old range. They still share the server’s CPU, memory and writer. A tenant query without a date restriction may need every month. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=partition-pruning">partition-pruning lesson</a> makes that distinction visible in real PostgreSQL plans.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="copies"><h2>Copies for reads</h2>
   <p>A cache stores an answer or a representation that the application can reuse. A product page may ask for the same description thousands of times between edits. Returning a cached description avoids asking the database to assemble it again. A cache miss still needs the source, so its normal hit rate and its behaviour after a restart both matter.</p>
   <p>The application must decide how a changed description reaches the cache: invalidate it, replace it, or allow a bounded period of staleness. A time-to-live limits how long an entry is retained under that policy; it does not prove the underlying source has stayed unchanged. A burst of simultaneous misses can send the saved work back to the database all at once. Coalescing duplicate fills, limiting their concurrency and testing an empty cache are part of the design.</p>
   <p>For checkout, reading cached stock is not a reservation. The authoritative writer must still decide whether stock can be taken. It is reasonable for a browsing page to show an item that sold out a moment ago if checkout handles that result correctly. It is a different promise to accept the purchase based solely on the cached number.</p>
   <p>A <strong>read replica</strong> maintains another database copy and can serve queries against it. In a common PostgreSQL arrangement, A accepts writes and streams its change log to B. Catalogue queries can go to B; order creation still goes to A. B has its own query-serving resources, but also spends resources receiving and applying changes. This arrangement is described in PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html">standby documentation</a>.</p>
   <figure class="copy-figure"><div class="copy-grid"><div><h3>A · writer</h3><p>Tenant 7<br />Tenant 8</p><small>Checkout writes<br />Reads needing the writer</small></div><div class="connection"><span>Changes from A → B</span></div><div><h3>B · read copy</h3><p>Tenant 7<br />Tenant 8</p><small>Eligible catalogue reads<br />Change replay</small></div></div><figcaption>The data is copied, not divided. Moving reads to B can free resources on A, but each checkout still reaches A and its changes must reach B.</figcaption></figure>
   <p>Now follow a customer who edits their delivery address on A, receives success, then loads their profile from B. If B has not applied the change, the old address can return. Routing the immediate read to the writer, or waiting for an appropriate replica progress marker, can preserve a read-your-write requirement. Sending it to an arbitrary healthy replica cannot. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">Replication and failover</a> develops the distinction between a copied change, a readable change and a safe replacement writer.</p>
   <p>Read copies can also serve reporting, but long standby queries may conflict with applying changes needed for recovery. PostgreSQL may cancel a conflicting query or delay replay according to configuration; feedback that retains older versions can increase cleanup pressure on the writer. A reporting copy and a failover candidate can therefore have different priorities. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/reporting/">reporting article</a> explains when a summary or a different analytical store is a better next step.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ownership"><h2>Divide the writes</h2>
   <p>When one writer remains the limit, independent parts of the workload can sometimes be assigned to different databases. This is <strong>sharding</strong>: a routing rule gives each subset an owner. With tenant-based sharding, tenant 7’s orders and stock might belong to A, while tenant 8’s belong to B. A tenant-local checkout then goes to its owner and can remain a local transaction.</p>
   <figure class="copy-figure"><div class="shard-grid"><div><h3>Shard A</h3><p>Tenant 7</p><small>Tenant 7 reads and writes</small></div><div><h3>Shard B</h3><p>Tenant 8</p><small>Tenant 8 reads and writes</small></div></div><figcaption>These are different authoritative subsets. Each shard may also need replicas for recovery. Adding write owners and adding copies solve separate problems.</figcaption></figure>
   <p>The key is useful when it follows the application’s boundaries. Orders, lines and tenant-specific products can travel together under tenant ID. A query naming tenant 7 can be sent to one owner. A report covering every tenant needs work from several owners, followed by a combined answer. A rule requiring globally unique catalogue codes is no longer protected merely by each shard’s local unique constraint.</p>
   <p>Tenant 8 also exposes the limit of this split. If that one tenant consumes most of the system, moving it to an otherwise empty shard may provide isolation, but adding ten shards for other tenants will not divide tenant 8’s own work. Splitting inside the tenant may help, at the cost of making some of its operations cross the new boundary. A key chosen only to distribute bytes evenly can scatter the records that a checkout needs together.</p>
   <p>Some distributed SQL systems coordinate transactions across owners as part of the engine; an application-managed set of ordinary PostgreSQL servers does not acquire that capability merely because it shares a router. Distributed coordination can preserve a useful transaction contract while adding network work and dependencies on several participants. Inspect the actual engine’s rules for transactions, constraints and failure recovery. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/partitioning-and-sharding/">Partitioning and sharding</a> follows routing, co-location and the safe movement of a tenant between owners.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="clusters"><h2>What a cluster shares</h2>
   <p>“Cluster” says that several components cooperate; it does not tell us which component accepts a write or what the extra nodes contain. In PostgreSQL administration the same word can even mean the collection of databases managed by one server instance. Draw the arrangement before inferring its capacity.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Database arrangements and remaining shared work"><table><thead><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Where the next checkout goes</th><th>What remains shared</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One larger server</td><td>The same writer</td><td>Its resource budget and conflicting records</td></tr><tr><td>Writer plus read replicas</td><td>The writer</td><td>The write stream and writer’s decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Several compute nodes using shared storage</td><td>The service’s designated write path</td><td>The storage service and its coordination rules</td></tr><tr><td>Independent shards</td><td>The owner selected by the key</td><td>Routing metadata and any cross-shard operation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>A shared-storage service may replicate its storage internally, so “shared” does not necessarily mean one physical disk. It does mean extra query nodes are not automatically extra independent write owners. Conversely, an arrangement with independent storage can still require several nodes to agree before a write succeeds. Ask what resource a request uses and what acknowledgements it must wait for.</p>
   <p>The same questions prevent misleading capacity totals. Ten nodes with a terabyte each do not necessarily provide ten terabytes of distinct application data if each logical record has three copies. A reader node’s spare CPU does not let it accept arbitrary writes unless the architecture gives it that authority. Count useful data and useful work after replication, coordination and reserved recovery capacity.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure"><h2>Capacity after a failure</h2>
   <p>An arrangement must be sized for the failures it promises to survive. If two read replicas normally share catalogue traffic, losing one sends more reads somewhere else. The survivor may also need to become the writer. If it was already near its limit, a correct promotion can still leave the service unable to meet its latency target.</p>
   <p>Follow the whole path when a node fails: detection, decision, stopping the old writer, promoting or electing the new one, routing fresh connections and resolving interrupted requests. A client whose commit reply disappeared may not know whether its purchase succeeded. It must recover the same operation identity rather than create another order. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling uncertain outcomes</a> supplies that application branch.</p>
   <p>A region failure changes which resources survive together. Two nodes in one region may lose power, networking or access to the same control service at once. A remote copy can survive that failure only to the extent that the needed data reached it and the surviving system can make a valid promotion decision. Waiting for a remote acknowledgement before success strengthens that data-survival contract, but also puts the remote path into normal commit latency and availability.</p>
   <p>For the shop, test both the normal workload and the promised degraded arrangements. Remove a reader and observe the remaining load. Exercise writer failover with ambiguous client outcomes. Test the remote recovery path with dependencies from the lost region unavailable. Check whether backup restoration, routing, credentials and enough spare capacity actually exist where recovery will run. Replication is not a substitute for a retained recovery history when a valid deletion is copied everywhere.</p>
   <p>The resulting choice may still be one well-used server with a tested standby. Or it may be a set of tenant owners, each with its own recovery copies. The decision is supported when the arrangement meets the stated workload and failure requirements, and the application can explain where a request goes while both are changing.</p>
  </section>
